\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The spinor time map}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{In a finite periodic grid #{\Lambda=\prod_{j=1}^n
\mathbb Z/N_j\mathbb Z}, #{n\ge1}, choose a time axis #{t}.
All periods are positive. Use the coordinate formula of the
\vocabk{site reflection}{fdip-007M} at this period:
#{\theta_s} negates #{x_t} and fixes the other coordinates.
Write #{(\theta_s^*\psi)(x)=\psi(\theta_s x)}.}

\p{Let #{S,F} be nonzero finite-dimensional complex Hermitian
spaces. On spin #{S} take self-adjoint represented directions
#{\varepsilon_j} with
#{\varepsilon_i\varepsilon_j+\varepsilon_j\varepsilon_i
=2\delta_{ij}\bbI_S}, and a self-adjoint grading #{\Gamma}
squaring to #{\bbI_S} and anticommuting with each direction,
as for the \vocabk{lift of the signed coordinate group}{fdip-016P}.
Supply conjugate-linear isometric bijections #{c:S\to S} and
#{c_F:F\to F}, with #{c\varepsilon_j c^{-1}=\varepsilon_j}
for every #{j}.}

\p{The spin fields take values in #{S\otimes F}, with the
tensor Hermitian inner product summed over sites. Let
#{\mathcal Q} send each value #{v\otimes w} to
#{cv\otimes c_Fw}, extended conjugate-linearly. With the inverse
of this same map define the \newvocab{spinor time map} by
##{S_t=(\Gamma\varepsilon_t\otimes\bbI_F)\,
\theta_s^*\,\mathcal Q^{-1}.}
Thus the value at #{\theta_s x} is conjugated and multiplied
by the spin action #{\Gamma\varepsilon_t} to give the new
value at #{x}.}
